---
import { renderCrsBlock } from "@crustini/syntax";
import "../styles/global.css";

const example = `app! {
  screen!(240, 135)
  fps!(30)

  state! {
    x: i16 = 120
    y: i16 = 67
    vx: i16 = 1
  }

  setup! {
    clear!(0)
  }

  update! {
    x = x + vx

    if x > 232 {
      vx = -1
    }

    if x < 8 {
      vx = 1
    }
  }

  draw! {
    clear!(12)
    circle!(x, y, 8, 255)
  }
}`;

const miniExample = `rx starter brick-breaker my-game
rx proof my-game/src/main.flour
rx bake my-game/src/main.flour`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content="Crustini is a tiny .flour language for playful apps that bakes into no_std Rust." />
    <title>Crustini - a tiny .flour language</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="topbar">
      <div class="shell topbar-inner">
        <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Crustini home">
          <span class="brand-mark">rx</span>
          <span>Crustini</span>
        </a>
        <nav class="nav" aria-label="Main">
          <a href="#language">Language</a>
          <a href="#bake">Bake</a>
          <a href="/live/">Docs Live</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="shell hero-grid">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">tiny source language for playful apps</p>
            <h1>Crustini</h1>
            <p class="lede">Write crisp <code>.flour</code> scripts for little games, toys, and tiny-screen experiments. Run them with <code>rx</code>, then bake them into boring <code>#![no_std]</code> Rust when it is time to ship.</p>
            <div class="actions">
              <a class="button primary" href="#bake">Bake a starter</a>
              <a class="button" href="/live/">Open Docs Live</a>
            </div>
            <div class="quick-strip" aria-label="Crustini language traits">
              <span>.flour files</span>
              <span>fixed update/draw loop</span>
              <span>no Rust ceremony</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="code-frame" aria-label="Crustini source example">
            <div class="code-label">
              <span>src/main.flour</span>
              <strong>live language shape</strong>
            </div>
            <Fragment set:html={renderCrsBlock(example)} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="language" class="section section-hot">
        <div class="shell split">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">language first</p>
            <h2>Small syntax. Fast loops. Actual pixels.</h2>
            <p>Crustini is not trying to hide a giant app stack behind cute names. A <code>.flour</code> file says what the screen is, what state exists, how <code>update</code> mutates it, and how <code>draw</code> paints it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="language-list" aria-label="Language highlights">
            <div><strong>Macro-shaped</strong><span>Simple blocks that stay easy to scan.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Game-loop native</strong><span><code>setup</code>, <code>update</code>, and <code>draw</code> are the main moves.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Embedded headed</strong><span>Generated Rust moves toward fixed pools, handles, and no default heap.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="bake" class="section">
        <div class="shell bake-grid">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">rx bake</p>
            <h2>From starter to artifact without killing the vibe.</h2>
            <p>A bake writes generated Rust into the bakery, then Cargo builds it against <code>crustini-core</code>. Use <code>proof</code> when you only want diagnostics, and <code>bake</code> when you want the artifact.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="terminal" aria-label="Build commands">
            {miniExample.split("\n").map((line) => <code><span>$</span>{line}</code>)}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="workspace" class="section">
        <div class="shell">
          <p class="eyebrow">what is inside</p>
          <h2>The bakery is small on purpose.</h2>
          <div class="grid">
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>rx CLI</h3>
              <p><code>crates/crustini</code> owns starters, project config, bakery writing, and Cargo orchestration.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>.flour language</h3>
              <p><code>crates/crustini-lang</code> parses the source app model and emits generated Rust.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>Runtime surface</h3>
              <p><code>crates/crustini-core</code> keeps the generated app API tiny and <code>#![no_std]</code>.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>Starters</h3>
              <p>Real runnable apps live in <code>examples/</code>; compiler fixtures stay in <code>fixtures/</code>.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile accent-tile">
              <h3>Docs Live</h3>
              <p>Draft language notes with highlighted <code>flour</code> blocks and copy the preview HTML.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>Compatibility</h3>
              <p><code>build</code> and <code>.crst</code> still exist as old aliases, but new copy should say <code>bake</code> and <code>.flour</code>.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="footer">
      <div class="shell">MIT licensed. Version 0.0.1.</div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
